<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <key>Label</key>
  <string>com.aetherclaude.pflog</string>

  <!--
    Persistent pf-block capture.

    pflog0 is pf's pseudo-interface that mirrors every packet matching
    a rule with the `log` keyword. The com.aetherclaude anchor's
    block-drop rules use `log`, so this tcpdump captures every TCP /
    UDP packet pf rejected for aetherclaude (UID 965) — the
    AetherClaude orchestrator's blocked egress.

    Output: /Users/Shared/aetherclaude/logs/pf-blocked.log
      - one line per blocked packet
      - -tttt = absolute ISO timestamp with µs
      - -nn   = no DNS / port-name resolution (faster, more diagnosable)
      - -s 200 = snap each packet to 200 bytes (headers only; full
                 payloads aren't useful for "what got blocked")
      - -l    = line-buffered output so the log updates immediately

    Rotation: com.aetherclaude.logrotate, nightly at 03:30. (This used to
    say "rely on macOS's newsyslog" — that was wrong. newsyslog only
    rotates paths with an explicit /etc/newsyslog.d entry and we never
    shipped one, so this log grew unbounded.) The file is line-buffered
    text so a tail -f works.

    Only root can read pflog0, so UserName is implicit-root.

    Wait for pflog0 before exec'ing tcpdump: at boot this job races
    com.aetherclaude.pf-enable, and tcpdump against a not-yet-created
    interface dies instantly with "(No such device exists)". KeepAlive
    did eventually recover it, but only after filling pf-blocked.err with
    1.2 MB of that one message. Poll for the interface for up to 60s
    instead, then exec so tcpdump keeps this job's PID.
  -->

  <key>ProgramArguments</key>
  <array>
    <string>/bin/bash</string>
    <string>-c</string>
    <string>for _ in $(seq 1 60); do /sbin/ifconfig pflog0 &gt;/dev/null 2&gt;&amp;1 &amp;&amp; break; sleep 1; done; exec /usr/sbin/tcpdump -i pflog0 -nn -tttt -s 200 -l</string>
  </array>

  <key>StandardOutPath</key>
  <string>/Users/Shared/aetherclaude/logs/pf-blocked.log</string>
  <key>StandardErrorPath</key>
  <string>/Users/Shared/aetherclaude/logs/pf-blocked.err</string>

  <key>RunAtLoad</key>
  <true/>

  <!--
    Restart on exit (tcpdump shouldn't die under normal load, but if
    it does we want to come right back up so we don't lose blocked
    packets). Throttle keeps a tight crash loop from flooding logs.
  -->
  <key>KeepAlive</key>
  <true/>
  <key>ThrottleInterval</key>
  <integer>10</integer>
</dict>
</plist>
